#!/usr/bin/env zsh

# wrong-cmd — the tripwire behind the dsh launchers' dangerous-command guard
# (dsh-agent / dsh-photocraft / dsh-jailbreak).
#
# Every guarded command name is a SYMLINK inside wrong-cmd.d/, and each launcher
# prepends that directory to PATH before it execs dsh. Most links point at
# this script; the subcommand-aware trio (git, npm, pnpm) points at the sibling
# wrong-cmd-subcmd instead, which looks at the subcommand word and lets every
# other invocation through unchanged. A PATH shim is the only interception that
# survives into the session's own commands: the bash tool spawns a
# non-interactive `bash -c`, and aliases are neither exported to it nor expanded
# by it. The shim therefore reaches the agent's shell calls, its background jobs
# and subagents, and the TUI's own `!` shell — which is where a wrong command
# would actually have run.
#
# Contract: this script IGNORES every argument. It never runs, forwards or
# even inspects the command it stands in for, so no flag can turn the guard
# back into the real thing. It names the command it was invoked as (argv[0],
# i.e. the symlink — not an argument), writes only to stderr, touches no file,
# and exits non-zero so the caller sees a failure instead of a silent success.
#
# Scope: PATH resolution only. An absolute path (/bin/rm), a rewritten PATH,
# a shell redirection (`> file`), `find -delete`, `node -e`, the harness's own
# file tools, any MCP server, and subcommand-level hazards (git clean -fdx,
# git reset --hard, git checkout -- .) are all outside it. This is a speed bump
# that tells the agent it went the wrong way, not a sandbox: the harness file
# sandbox and approval policy stay the authority.
#
# Maintained through the wrong-cmd.d/ listing: to guard another command, add a
# symlink there; to release one, remove it. Nothing else needs editing. Never
# link an interpreter this script itself needs (`zsh`, `env`, `sh`) — the guard
# would then block its own shebang.

name="${0:t}"

if [ "$name" = "wrong-cmd" ]; then
    cat >&2 <<'EOF'
wrong-cmd: you are running the wrong command.

This is the guard itself, invoked directly. It stands in for a dangerous
command only when reached through a name in wrong-cmd.d/ — the directory
dsh-agent / dsh-photocraft / dsh-jailbreak prepend to PATH for the session they
launch.
EOF
else
    printf 'wrong-cmd: %s is not available in this session — you are running the wrong command.\n\n' "'$name'" >&2
    cat >&2 <<EOF
The launcher pointed '$name' at this guard, so nothing ran and nothing changed.
Arguments are ignored on purpose: this is a tripwire, not the real command.
Every process this session spawns shares the guard, so this notice can also come
from a build tool or script that used '$name' internally.

Do not retry through an absolute path (/bin/…, /usr/bin/…), and do not rewrite
PATH to get around this: that is the same wrong command.

If the action is genuinely needed, use the supported route instead:
  * deleting a file or directory: check the resolved absolute target, then
    run `trash <abs path>` (the suite's cross-platform wrapper; if bin/ is
    not on PATH, run the repo's bin/trash);
  * ending a process or truncating a file: confirm with the user first;
  * remote shell, privilege or disk-level operations: ask the user to run it.
EOF
fi

exit 1
